request: Rename the `watched` field of watchlist items to `seen` everywhere.

governor turn 1: plan -> movie, watchlist-store, watchlist  (Pure field rename: the watchlist-item flag `watched` becomes `seen`. Only units that declare or directly use the field change: movie (declares it on Movie and WatchItem), watchlist-store (creates/toggles/persists it), watchlist (renders it). Non-field names (toggleWatched, watchedCount) stay, per the request's wording. Acceptance tests pin the new `seen` item shape.)
round 1: owners movie=ok watchlist-store=ok watchlist=rejected; oracle RED
governor turn 2: plan -> watchlist  (The field rename is already applied in movie and watchlist-store; only watchlist remains red, and its submission was rejected purely for the unapproved test edit. That edit is exactly what the rename requires (asserting `.seen` instead of `.watched` with unchanged intent), so I approve it and re-instruct watchlist to update its template and spec to the new `seen` field.)
round 2: owners watchlist=ok; oracle RED
governor turn 3: plan -> watchlist-store, watchlist  (The watched→seen rename is complete: tsc exits 0 and all unit specs pass. The three failing tests are governor acceptance tests with harness mistakes, not behaviour defects: WatchlistStore registers an effect() in its constructor, so it must be created inside an injection context (NG0203), and the Watchlist template's RouterLink needs router providers (NG0201). This round only replaces those two acceptance tests with corrected versions; no unit source changes are needed.)
round 3: owners watchlist-store=ok watchlist=ok; oracle GREEN
governor turn 4: accept  (Oracle is green (tsc exit 0, 44/44 tests pass). The watchlist item field `watched` is renamed to `seen` in the models (movie), storage and toggle logic (watchlist-store), and rendering (watchlist); the previously approved test assertion update in watchlist is applied and all acceptance tests confirm the new shape. Units that never referenced the field required no changes.)

